<Base title="About this book"><div class="page-pad about-content"><header class="page-header"><h1>A working mental model.</h1><p>Kubernetes becomes easier to reason about when you can see the relationships, the transitions, and the gap between what you asked for and what exists.</p></header><h2>Learn the mechanism</h2><p>This book connects practical operation with the internals that explain it. Each lesson starts with a problem, introduces the model, follows it through an interactive scene, and gives you a way to test your understanding locally.</p><p>The learning path spans foundations, intermediate operations, and advanced control-plane and platform patterns. You can move in sequence or use the concept atlas to investigate a specific question.</p><h2>Use the models deliberately</h2><p>Scenes are bounded educational models. Their timing, node counts, and implementation details are simplified where that makes a mechanism easier to inspect. The “About this model” note identifies material boundaries. Animation time is not a measured Kubernetes latency.</p><p>Real systems are asynchronous, eventually consistent, and implementation-dependent. The local labs help bridge the explanation and real behavior. Production HA, managed-cloud load balancers, storage drivers, and failure domains need environments beyond a small local cluster.</p><h2>Follow the primary sources</h2><p>Lessons link to upstream Kubernetes documentation and official project documentation. Each lesson records a review date and Kubernetes version. Stable core APIs are the baseline; ecosystem add-ons and experimental behavior are called out explicitly.</p><h2 id="correctness">Accuracy and review</h2><p>The full curriculum—60 lessons and 121 narrated scenes, including alternate paths—was checked against primary documentation on 27–28 October 2026. The reviewed baseline is Kubernetes 1.37; the reproducible local labs pin kind 0.33.0 and its Kubernetes 1.37.0 node image. Lesson review dates identify the content check, not an upstream certification.</p><p>Review includes explanations, diagram states, examples, and the distinction between baseline labs and optional extensions. Add-ons have independent support requirements: the Argo CD extension requires a separately verified compatible version pair. A documentation review cannot guarantee behavior on every runtime, network or storage provider. Follow the linked primary sources and report a concrete mismatch when you find one.</p><h2>Your progress belongs to you</h2><p>Completion, bookmarks, and your last lesson stay in this browser. There is no account, learning-data backend, or tracking script. Export your progress from the learning path to carry it to another browser. Clearing browser storage removes local history.</p><h2 id="contribute">Built to be improved</h2><p>Content, simulation scenarios, shared renderers, and labs have separate responsibilities. The repository includes authoring templates, a coverage validator, and a contribution guide. Improvements should preserve precise explanations, primary references, accessible visuals, and runnable exercises.</p><p>Public source publication and open-source licensing will be decided by the project owner. This site is an independent learning resource and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Kubernetes project or CNCF.</p><h2>Practice, then explain it back</h2><p>Before playing a scene, predict the next state. Change one input. Explain which component acts and why. In a lab, inspect the object’s spec, status, events, and ownership before changing it. Understanding the boundary is as useful as memorizing the command.</p><a class="button primary" href="/curriculum/">Open the learning path →</a></div></Base>
